Enhance an existing Code Insight Platform with an end-to-end, multi-layer pipeline that ingests any Git repository, builds a rich knowledge model, and generates a full suite of enterprise-grade documents via AWS Bedrock agents — with consistent formatting, high confidence, and zero hallucination of capabilities.

Layer 1 — Static Analysis (ground truth extraction)
Parse source code across Java (JavaParser, PMD, SpotBugs, OpenRewrite), .NET (Roslyn), Python (AST, Radon), and TypeScript (ts-morph) into a normalized model covering services, controllers, repositories, entities, and dependencies.
Extract build metadata from Maven pom.xml, Gradle, .csproj, and package.json — capturing framework versions, libraries, Java/runtime version, and plugins. Analyze infrastructure files (Dockerfile, docker-compose, Jenkinsfile, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Helm Charts, Kubernetes YAML, OpenShift templates) to discover deployment architecture, runtime requirements, and external services.

Layer 2 — Repository Knowledge Model
Construct a component-relationship graph (service → controller → repository → database table) and store it in PostgreSQL as the canonical metadata store. Every downstream Bedrock agent must read exclusively from this structured metadata — never from raw source files.
Each repository record includes: languages, frameworks, API count, service/entity counts, external systems (Oracle, Kafka, Redis, Active Directory), security findings, quality findings, and technical debt items.
Optionally index the graph in NetworkX or Neo4j for traversal queries.

Layer 3 — Multi-Agent Bedrock (structured JSON I/O)
Five specialized agents each receive scoped JSON metadata as input and return structured JSON only (no prose, no Markdown):
AgentPrimary outputsArchitecture agentExecutive summary, architecture style, components, data flow, deployment modelRequirements agentFunctional requirements (inferred from endpoints, schema, workflow states), technical requirementsSecurity agentVulnerability findings, CVEs, misconfiguration risksModernization agentCurrent-state assessment, target state, migration complexity, effort estimateDocumentation agentAPI docs, data model descriptions, sequence diagrams (Mermaid), support runbook
Prompt pattern: Always supply a structured JSON context object and instruct: "Use only the provided repository metadata. Do not invent capabilities. Return valid JSON matching this schema."
                No "creative" instructions, Do not invent capabilities. If information is unavailable, state that explicitly.

Layer 4 — Validation & Document Models
Deserialize every Bedrock response through Pydantic models (e.g. ArchitectureDocument, SecurityAssessment). Reject or retry any response that fails schema validation. This ensures downstream processing is fully deterministic.

Layer 5 — Document Generation
Render validated models through Jinja2 templates into target formats:

DOCX (python-docx) for enterprise delivery
PDF / HTML for web/portal distribution
Markdown → Confluence REST API for wiki publishing
Mermaid / PlantUML for diagrams
CSV for security and debt findings

Critical design rule: Bedrock generates JSON. Templates generate documents. The LLM never writes the final formatted output.
